course: medicine
lesson: 48
title: Broken bones in sport: fingers, wrists and collarbones
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Children's bones are bendier than adults', a bit like a green twig: they can bend and crack on one side only, but they still break and still need a doctor.
explain: Dev dives for a catch at cricket in Leicester and lands on his hand, «It's only an ache, I'm batting next», but landing on an outstretched hand is the most common way children break a wrist, and a small break can feel just like a sprain.
explain: A coach's check list for a break: heard a crack, the limb looks out of shape, you can't use it, pain right on the bone, or swelling fast; even one or two of these means a doctor should look.
explain: Never pull, pop or push a bone back like on telly: keep it still the way you found it, support it, and let an adult get help.
explain: At A&E an X-ray takes a picture of the bones with a beam you can't feel, so the scariest part is just keeping still for a moment.
fact: Children's bones can crack on one side and bend, called a greenstick break, but they can still break fully and need treatment like adults' bones.
fact: Falling onto an outstretched hand is the most common way children break a wrist, and a small «buckle» break can feel like a sprain.
fact: Signs of a broken bone include a crack heard, an odd shape, not being able to use the limb, pain right on the bone and fast swelling; people can often still move a broken bone.
fact: A jammed finger that looks bent may be broken or dislocated, and pulling it straight can damage the bone, joint or nerves more.
fact: For a suspected break: keep it still as you found it, support it, put something cold wrapped in cloth near it, and an adult gets help.
fact: Someone who may need an operation should not eat or drink until a doctor says so.
fact: The collarbone often breaks from a fall on the shoulder, and most broken collarbones are treated with a sling, not a cast.
fact: A broken leg, a bone poking through the skin or heavy bleeding needs an ambulance, so an adult calls 999 in the UK or 911 in the US.
fact: An X-ray doesn't hurt; you just keep still while the picture is taken.
fact: A break can still be found and treated the next day, so swelling that is worse the morning after is a reason to get checked.
fact: After a cast comes off the bone is still getting stronger and the muscles are weak, so contact sport waits for the doctor's plan.
fact: Many broken little toes heal at home, but a broken big toe, a toe out of shape or one that won't stop hurting should be checked.
fact: A baby has about 300 bones and an adult 206, because many join together as you grow.
fact: In Northern Ireland «wise up» means don't be silly, and in Britain «bricking it» means being very scared.
exercise: Can children's bones break? | Yes, they bend and can crack or break | No, they only ever bend | No, children have no real bones | Only after age sixteen
exercise: What is the most common way children break a wrist? | Falling onto an outstretched hand | Writing too much at school with a tight grip | Sleeping on the arm at night for too long | Catching a ball with two hands
exercise: Which is a sign a bone may be broken? | The limb looks out of shape | The skin feels a bit cold | You feel slightly hungry | The limb has a small freckle
exercise: If you can still move it, is it definitely not broken? | No, people can often move a broken bone | Yes, being able to move it proves that it's fine | Yes, unless it's your leg | No, moving it always breaks it more
exercise: A friend's finger looks bent after catching a ball. What should you do? | Keep it still and get an adult | Pull it hard and fast to straighten it out | Shake it about to wake it up again | Tape it bent to the next one and keep playing
exercise: What's the first aid for a possible broken bone? | Keep it still as you found it and get help | Push it back into shape gently | Walk on it to see if it holds | Rub it until the pain stops
exercise: Why no food or drink after a bad break? | An operation might be needed | Food makes bones weaker | Drinks make the swelling worse | The hospital wants you hungry
exercise: How are most broken collarbones treated? | With a sling | With a full body cast | With a metal bar every time | With nothing at all
exercise: A bone is poking through the skin. How should the child get to hospital? | By ambulance, an adult calls 999 | In a parent's car, because it's always quicker | On the bus with a friend | Walking slowly with help
exercise: Does an X-ray hurt? | No, you can't feel it | Yes, like a sharp sting | Yes, it burns like hot water | Only when it's on the wrists
exercise: The wrist is more swollen the morning after a fall. What now? | Tell an adult and get it checked | Wait a month to see if it goes down | It's too late to see a doctor about it now | Wrap it tight and play
exercise: The cast is off after 4 weeks. Can you tackle on Saturday? | Not until the doctor's plan says so | Yes, cast off means the bone is fully healed | Yes, but only twice | Yes, the bone is extra strong now
exercise: Which broken toe should be checked by a doctor? | A broken big toe or a toe out of shape | None, toes always heal alone | Only the little toe | Only if it's painted
exercise: Why does a baby have more bones than an adult? | Many join together as you grow | Adults lose bones in accidents | Babies have extra fingers | Bones melt away with age
exercise: What is a greenstick break? | A bone that bends and cracks on one side | A bone painted green in a cast | A break that comes from falling out of a tree | A bone with no crack at all
test: Ruby's netball finger looks bent. Mate: «Wise up, just pull it straight». Should she? | No, keep it still and get an adult | Yes, a quick pull fixes it | Yes, but only by the coach | No, she should bend it the other way
test: Dev: «only an ache, I'm batting next» after landing on his hand. What's best? | Tell an adult; a wrist break can feel like an ache | Bat with the other hand and see | Ignore it until next season | Ice it for a minute then bat
test: Jayden's got a lump on his collarbone and holds his arm tight to his body. What should happen? | Support the arm and an adult takes him to A&E | Swing the arm round to test it | Back into the scrum after a drink | Pull the shoulder to pop it in
test: Mia has 3 of the coach's 5 signs: crack, odd shape, pain on the bone. What now? | A doctor checks it | She waits for all 5 signs | She plays on, 3 is not many | Her mum rubs it better
test: Kai in Perth: «kids' bones don't break, they just bend, sweet as». Right? | No, they bend and crack and need treating | Yes, kids' bones are rubber | Yes, until they turn ten | No, kids' bones break more easily than glass does
test: A dad says «pop Ewan's ankle back in, seen it on telly». Ewan wants crisps. What's right? | Keep it still, no food or drink, an adult gets help | Pop it in and give him crisps | Let Ewan walk it off with crisps | Pull it gently and give him a fizzy drink
test: A bone shows through a boy's skin. A parent says «I'll drive him, quicker». What's better? | Call 911 or 999 for an ambulance | Drive fast with the windows down | Wait for half-time to decide | Let him sit on the bench first
test: Priya at A&E: «Will the X-ray hurt? I'm bricking it». What's true? | It doesn't hurt; she just keeps still | It hurts a bit, like an injection does | It burns the skin for a moment | It only hurts if she moves about
test: Chloe: «just mid pain, happened yesterday anyway». Too late for a doctor? | No, a break can still be checked the next day | Yes, after 12 hours it's much too late to check it | Yes, broken bones fix themselves | No, but only if it's still bleeding a lot
test: Thabo in Cape Town: «Ag, nothing you can do for a broken toe». His big toe got stamped on. True? | No, a broken big toe should be checked | Yes, nothing at all can ever be done for toes | Yes, toes have no proper bones in them | No, he must have it removed
test: Theo: «Cast's off, I'm tackling Saturday!» What would happen if he did? | The weak bone and muscles could be hurt again | The bone would get stronger faster from the tackles | Nothing, a healed bone can't break again | His cast would grow back
exam: Identify the most frequent mechanism by which a child sustains a fracture of the wrist. | A fall upon an outstretched hand | Prolonged writing with a pencil at school | Sleeping upon the affected limb | Catching a ball with both hands
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bones of children. | They are more pliable but remain susceptible to fracture | They are wholly incapable of fracture | They are more brittle than those of adults | They contain no living tissue whatsoever
exam: It is given that a bone protrudes through the skin of a child's leg. State the appropriate course of action. | An ambulance is to be summoned by an adult | The child is to be conveyed by private car | The bone is to be pushed back into place | The child is to be assisted to walk to the bench
exam: Ascertain the treatment most commonly employed for a fracture of the collarbone. | The arm is supported in a sling | The entire torso is encased in plaster | The bone is manipulated by a teammate | No support of any kind is provided
